      "math_academy_id": "e-1361",
      "problem": "Simplify $\\sqrt{x} \\cdot \\sqrt{x^3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sqrt{x} \\cdot \\sqrt{x^3} &= \\\n\t\t\\sqrt{x \\cdot x^3} &= \\\n\t\t\\sqrt{x^4} &= \\\n\t\t|x^2|\n\t}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{x}^{2}| & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
